A full grown large dog/cat can make a huge difference in early 
battles, retreat into and around your pet.  Take cover through a 
doorway or a corridor.  If you know you are up against some tough 
monsters, (E)ngrave Elbereth early. As you are aware, it won't 
always work the first, even the second time, so start writing 
early if you think you are gonna need it.  An early wand of 
dig/fire makes for a great Engraving tool, lightning too if you 
can cure blindness quick (spell, carrot, !oEH, unihorn)

A huge improvement to my games came when I began to play 
'slower' such that I gained as little experience as I could avoid 
and focused on finding some nice gear.  A pet or two can watch 
your back while you are less than Xlevel 5, and you won't be 
seeing any soldier ants till much deeper.  Focus on getting your 
AC down, finding new spells and tools.  Remember and use the 
things you find, every trick up your sleeve counts.  I have this 
Rogue game I started earlier this week, found a flute in the 
Mines early on, came back up to go down to Sokoban... And I'm 
toe-to-toe with a Werewolf and his small army of (winter)wolves.  
Earlier I'd priced the flute as magical, so I played it for the 
canines and put them all asleep.  Nothing else in my inv would've 
saved me besides that flute, or a lucky shot with Elbereth.

With experience, you'll realize the danger of a situation much 
earlier, pay attention to your surroundings and don't bet on any 
longshots.  If your HP has gotten low, be extra careful until its 
back up to a safe range, and as a monk, never forget about your 
spells.  Pricing spellbooks is invaluable, anything cheap you 
should have a good chance of reading early, and have a pet make 
sure it isnt cursed.  Have a pet to make sure everything isnt 
cursed.  Never know when that Goblin's orcish helm might be +4, 
but if you don't test it first, you can bet its cursed -2 heheh.

Anyway, thems is my tips.  Good luck.

